window.MT1885_STORIES = {"stops":{"stop-01-mollie-smith":{"advisory":null,"sections":[{"heading":null,"text":"You're standing at the corner of West 6th and Bowie, in the Market District — glass, retail, and the Whole Foods flagship filling the south side of the block. In 1884 this was 901 West Pecan Street, the home of insurance man Walter Hall, with servants' quarters attached at the rear. Nothing of that house survives, but this corner is where Austin's year of fear began.","lore":false},{"heading":"Who she was","text":"Mollie Smith was about twenty-five, born in Virginia, and had recently come to Austin from Waco. She worked as a cook for the Hall household and lived in the quarters behind the house with Walter Spencer, the man she shared her life with. She had a ten-year-old son, George. Like most of the women attacked in the year that followed, she was Black, she worked in service, and she slept only steps from the family she cooked for — close enough to be counted on, and far enough to be unprotected.","lore":false},{"heading":"The night of December 30–31, 1884","text":"Sometime in the night, an intruder entered the quarters and attacked Mollie and Walter Spencer as they slept. Spencer, who suffered serious head wounds, survived and raised the alarm. Mollie was not in the room. She was found the next morning in the yard near the outbuildings; she had been carried outside and killed with an axe. It was the first murder in a series no one yet knew was a series — Austin woke on New Year's Eve to a crime it had no framework to understand.","lore":false},{"heading":"What followed","text":"Police looked close to home, as they would all year: they arrested William \"Lem\" Brooks, a former boyfriend, but the evidence did not hold and he was released. Through the spring of 1885 the city recorded a wave of nighttime prowlings, break-ins, and assaults on servant women — and Galloway's research documents attacks on Black women as early as the summer of 1884. Mollie was buried in the segregated \"colored grounds\" of Oakwood Cemetery, in a grave that is unmarked today. What became of her son George, the record does not say.","lore":false}],"survivors":["Walter Spencer"],"siteToday":"No trace of the Hall house remains; the corner of West 6th and Bowie is now Market District retail and offices, with the Whole Foods Market flagship on the south side of the block.","loreNotes":[]},"stop-02-eliza-shelley":{"advisory":null,"sections":[{"heading":null,"text":"The corner in front of you — East 3rd and San Jacinto, deep in the convention-center district — was the corner of Cypress Street and San Jacinto in 1885. Behind Dr. Lucien B. Johnson's residence here stood a small cabin where a family lived. Construction fencing may surround you, since this whole district is being rebuilt through about 2029, so hold the older geography in mind: a doctor's house, a yard, and a cabin at the back.","lore":false},{"heading":"Who she was","text":"Eliza Shelley was about thirty, a cook for Dr. Johnson's family. She lived in the cabin behind the house with her three young sons, raising them alone while her husband, Ike, served a term in the Huntsville penitentiary. Cooking for one household while keeping another — her own — running single-handed, she was one of the hundreds of Black women whose labor kept the homes of 1880s Austin functioning, and whose safety, it would turn out, the city could not or would not secure.","lore":false},{"heading":"The night of May 6–7, 1885","text":"In the early hours of May 7, an intruder entered the cabin where Eliza and her boys were sleeping and killed her with an axe; her children, lying nearby, survived. It had been five months since Mollie Smith's murder — long enough for the city to file that crime away as a private tragedy. This one was harder to dismiss. Two servant women murdered in their own quarters within half a year began, slowly, to look like a pattern.","lore":false},{"heading":"What followed","text":"Police briefly held Ike Plummer, a young, mentally impaired man, on circumstantial evidence, then released him — the first of many arrests that led nowhere. By that same month, Galloway's research shows, nighttime disturbances and attacks were occurring almost nightly across Austin. By the end of 1885, contemporary reports claimed that roughly four hundred men had been arrested or questioned in connection with the panic, many of them Black; no one was finally convicted of any of the murders. Eliza was buried in the segregated grounds of Oakwood Cemetery; her three boys survived that night, and lost their mother in it.","lore":false}],"survivors":["Eliza Shelley's three young sons"],"siteToday":"The corner now holds garage-podium commercial buildings in the convention-center district, an area under reconstruction for the Convention Center rebuild through about 2029.","loreNotes":[{"title":"\"Servant Girl Annihilators\": a joke that became a name","text":"Three days after Eliza Shelley's murder, on May 10, 1885, a young Austin clerk named William Sydney Porter — later famous as the writer O. Henry — joked in a letter to a friend that the town was \"fearfully dull, except for the frequent raids of the Servant Girl Annihilators.\" No newspaper of 1885 used the phrase; it attached itself to the case only decades later. Historians such as Skip Hollandsworth prefer the label \"Midnight Assassin,\" a later and now common historical term, and this tour speaks, as Austin's own papers did, of the servant girl murders."}]},"stop-03-mary-ramey":{"advisory":"This stop concerns the murder of an eleven-year-old child, Mary Ramey, and is told with deliberate restraint.","sections":[{"heading":null,"text":"You're on East 4th Street at San Jacinto, one block north of Eliza Shelley's stop, in front of the Residence Inn tower — please stay on the public sidewalk, clear of the hotel drive. In 1885 this was 300 East Cedar Street, the home of Valentine O. Weed, with servants' quarters and a wash house at the rear of the lot. Two blocks northwest, the Driskill Hotel was rising that year — a city building its future while its servants slept afraid.","lore":false},{"heading":"Who she was","text":"Contemporary newspapers reported Mary as eleven; census records suggest she was born in Austin in 1875, which would have made her about ten. She is the youngest person in this story. She lived in the servants' quarters here with her mother, Rebecca, who worked for the Weed household. Her family was rooted deep in Black Austin: her uncle, the grocer Edward H. Carrington, was one of the community's most prominent business leaders, and his store building still stands as an East Austin landmark. Remember her as a child of this city, with a family that loved her.","lore":false},{"heading":"The night of August 30, 1885","text":"Before dawn, an intruder entered the quarters, struck Rebecca Ramey unconscious, and carried Mary to the wash house behind the home, where she was assaulted and fatally wounded. That is as much as this tour will say; the rest belongs to the historical record, not to a sidewalk. Sources differ on whether Mary died on August 30 or the 31st. Rebecca survived, but by her family's account she never recovered emotionally from that morning.","lore":false},{"heading":"What followed","text":"No one was ever brought to trial for Mary's murder. What investigators did record were bare footprints at the scene missing a toe — four toes on the right foot. In February 1886, a nineteen-year-old cook named Nathan Elgin was shot by a police officer farther east along this same Cedar Street, in the Masontown freedmen's community, while violently dragging a woman from a saloon; he died the next day, and the autopsy showed his right foot was missing a toe. Elgin was never charged and was killed without a trial — he remains a suspect, not an answer. Mary rests in the segregated grounds of Oakwood Cemetery.","lore":false}],"survivors":["Rebecca Ramey (her mother)"],"siteToday":"The Residence Inn Austin Downtown hotel tower now occupies the address; view it from the public sidewalk, clear of the hotel drive.","loreNotes":[]},"stop-04-susan-hancock":{"advisory":null,"sections":[{"heading":null,"text":"You're on East Cesar Chavez between Brazos and San Jacinto, at the landscaped edge of the Four Seasons grounds, with Lady Bird Lake just below. In 1885 this was Water Street, and the Hancock house at 203 East Water backed down the slope toward the Colorado River. After 140 years of renumbering, the exact parcel is uncertain — treat this stretch of the block as the place, and cross Cesar Chavez only at the Brazos or San Jacinto signals.","lore":false},{"heading":"Who she was","text":"Susan Clementine Hancock, born Scaggs in Alabama around 1840, was in her mid-forties. She and her husband Moses, a carpenter, had moved to Austin only months earlier and settled here near the river with their daughters, Lena and Ida. In the eyes of 1885 Austin she was also something the earlier victims were not: white and middle class. That should have made no difference to how hard the city looked for a killer — but it did, and that difference is part of this story too.","lore":false},{"heading":"The night of December 24, 1885","text":"According to Moses Hancock's account, just before midnight on Christmas Eve an intruder struck Susan with an axe as she slept in her daughter's bed and dragged her to the backyard. Moses woke, chased the man off, and carried her inside. Their daughters were away at a Christmas party. Susan died of her wounds four days later, on December 28; the autopsy performed that day by Dr. W.J. Burt survives in the historical record. And within about an hour of this attack, across town, another household was broken into — the night was not over.","lore":false},{"heading":"What followed","text":"Suspicion turned on Moses Hancock. Prosecutors leaned on a letter in which Susan wrote that she might leave him over his drinking; his trial ended in a hung jury, he was never retried, and by 1887 he stood exonerated. The two Christmas Eve attacks broke the city's composure: mass meetings, a Committee of Safety, armed volunteer patrols, a midnight curfew, saloons forced to close, an expanded police force, and the city marshal replaced by ex-Ranger James E. Lucy. The attacks stopped. Susan rests in Oakwood Cemetery, Section 2, Lot 459 — one of the few victims whose grave is marked.","lore":false}],"survivors":[],"siteToday":"High-rise hotel frontage and the landscaped Four Seasons grounds now run from Cesar Chavez down to the Ann and Roy Butler Hike-and-Bike Trail on Lady Bird Lake.","loreNotes":[{"title":"Lore: Jack the Ripper and the Pearl House","text":"In the autumn of 1888, as the Whitechapel murders gripped London, American newspapers began speculating that Austin's killer had crossed the Atlantic. The Austin version centered on \"Maurice,\" a Malay cook at the Pearl House hotel, which stood near the foot of Congress Avenue a few blocks west of this stop. He worked there in 1885, left town in January 1886, and reportedly talked of going to London — and the Statesman claimed most of the victims had lived \"in the immediate neighborhood\" of the hotel. London police really did question sailors and even visiting American cowboys. Historians treat the whole connection as period speculation with no evidentiary support: irresistible lore, and only that."}]},"stop-05-eula-phillips":{"advisory":null,"sections":[{"heading":null,"text":"You're at the northeast corner of West 8th and Guadalupe, at the top of the hill, where a parking structure now stands. In 1885 this was 302 West Hickory Street — three connected dwellings belonging to James Phillips Sr., at the corner of the block. Look one block north to the 1870s Hirshfeld House and Cottage on West 9th: they are this block's only survivors, and the truest picture left of what the street looked like that Christmas.","lore":false},{"heading":"Who she was","text":"Eula Phillips, born Eula Burditt in Travis County in 1868, was seventeen. Married at fourteen to Jimmy Phillips Jr., a carpenter's son, she was the mother of a baby boy, Tommy, and lived here in her in-laws' crowded household. The trial that followed her death would drag her name through Austin's press, so hold on to the plain facts first: she was a teenage mother, asleep beside her husband and her child, in a house full of family, on Christmas morning.","lore":false},{"heading":"The night of December 24, 1885","text":"Around 1 a.m. on Christmas morning — within an hour of the attack on Susan Hancock across town — the prevailing serial-murder theory holds that an intruder took Eula from the room where she slept beside Jimmy and the baby. She was killed in the backyard near the alley; Jimmy was found wounded in the bed. The attacker fled west up the alley toward Guadalupe, the street at your back. Two households in one night: Christmas 1885 is when Austin's long year of fear finally became open panic.","lore":false},{"heading":"What followed","text":"Jimmy Phillips was tried for Eula's murder in 1886 and convicted after sensational testimony that she had secretly visited May Tobin's \"assignation house\" near the Capitol; the Texas Court of Appeals threw the conviction out, and he was never retried. Key to his defense was a bloody bare footprint near the scene with four toes, matching neither Phillips nor Moses Hancock — and sharing, his lawyers argued, the distinctive missing little toe of Nathan Elgin's right foot, the young cook killed by police in February 1886 and found to be missing a toe. Elgin was never charged, and the case remains formally unsolved. Eula lies in an unmarked grave in Oakwood's Old Grounds; Tommy was raised by relatives.","lore":false}],"survivors":["James \"Jimmy\" Phillips Jr. (her husband)"],"siteToday":"A parking structure occupies the corner today; the preserved 1870s Hirshfeld House and Cottage on West 9th are the block's only surviving houses.","loreNotes":[]},"stop-06-irene-cross":{"advisory":null,"sections":[{"heading":null,"text":"You're on East 17th Street between San Jacinto and Trinity, inside the state's Capitol Complex — and across the street stands Scholz Garten, pouring beer since 1866, the only business on this tour that was open in 1885. This was Linden Street then, and a rear building at number 302 stood just east of San Jacinto.","lore":false},{"heading":"Who she was","text":"Irene Cross was in her mid-to-late thirties, born in Mississippi around 1847 — sources differ on her exact age. She worked as a servant and lived in the rear building on the Witman property here, with family close by, including her eight-year-old nephew, Douglas Brown. She had a son, Washington Cross, whose descendants remained in Austin well into the twentieth century — a family line that long outlasted the city's memory of what happened at this address.","lore":false},{"heading":"The night of May 22–23, 1885","text":"Late that night an intruder with a large knife attacked Irene in her bed; she survived a few hours, and died of her wounds. Her nephew Douglas Brown saw the attacker and described him — a heavyset, barefoot man with his trousers rolled up — a child's testimony, preserved in the blunt language of the period's press. The barefoot detail would echo through the rest of the case, when bare footprints became evidence at later scenes.","lore":false},{"heading":"What followed","text":"No one was ever charged with Irene's murder. It happened in the very weeks when, by Galloway's account, nighttime attacks and disturbances were occurring almost nightly across Austin — and it produced no lasting arrest, no trial, and little newspaper ink compared with what would be spent later that year. Irene was buried in the segregated grounds of Oakwood Cemetery, unmarked. Across the street, Scholz Garten went on serving; it is a strange, useful thing to stand where the one surviving business of 1885 faces the erased address of one of 1885's victims.","lore":false}],"survivors":["Douglas Brown (her nephew, who witnessed the attack)"],"siteToday":"The block has been absorbed into the Texas Capitol Complex and now holds State Parking Garage R, while Scholz Garten, open since 1866, still operates across the street.","loreNotes":[]},"stop-07-vance-washington":{"advisory":null,"sections":[{"heading":null,"text":"You're on the Drag — Guadalupe Street at West 24th — with the University of Texas across the street and the UT Tower rising to the southeast where Old Main once stood. In 1885 this was the northern edge of Austin, open and farm-like, and William B. Dunham's place kept a cabin and stable out here. Close by stood Wheatville, a freedmen's community built by Black Austinites after emancipation and later erased as the university district grew — hold both losses in mind at this stop.","lore":false},{"heading":"Who they were","text":"Gracie Vance was about twenty, a domestic worker; Orange Washington, a laborer, had come to Texas from Virginia. They lived together in the cabin behind Dunham's place, and on the last night of their lives they had company: Lucinda Boddy and Patsy Gibson were staying over. Gracie's mother, Eliza Vance, a washerwoman, lived on in Austin until about 1920 — surviving her daughter by some thirty-five years. This is the only stop on the tour where two people were murdered: a household, not just a victim.","lore":false},{"heading":"The night of September 27–28, 1885","text":"The attacker came in through the cabin window. Orange Washington was fatally struck as he slept; Lucinda Boddy and Patsy Gibson were beaten and left for dead, though both survived; Gracie was carried over the fence to the stable about a hundred yards away and killed there. Four people attacked in a single night, at the loneliest site in the whole series — a cabin at the edge of town, far from any neighbor who might have heard and come.","lore":false},{"heading":"What followed","text":"Lucinda Boddy, dazed from her injuries, first named a man called Dock Woods; he and another Black man, Oliver Townsend, were arrested, and neither was ever indicted for the murders — the pattern of the whole year in miniature. A note on the ground itself: period records disagree by a block or two, between the city directory's 2408 Guadalupe and a newspaper's San Marcos Street address; the best research places the cabin here. Gracie and Orange were the fifth and sixth people murdered. Three quiet months followed — and then came Christmas Eve.","lore":false}],"survivors":["Lucinda Boddy","Patsy Gibson"],"siteToday":"The address is now a student-retail strip on the Drag facing the University of Texas campus, where the UT Tower stands on the site of Old Main.","loreNotes":[]},"stop-08-oakwood-memorial":{"advisory":null,"sections":[{"heading":null,"text":"Oakwood Cemetery opens before you at 1601 Navasota Street — Austin's City Cemetery in 1885, about a mile and a quarter east of the Capitol. This is not a walking leg of the tour: most visitors arrive by car or ride, and it is best visited in daylight. Nearly every person in the story you have been following was brought here in the end, and this last stop belongs to them, not to their killer.","lore":false},{"heading":"Who rests here","text":"Susan Hancock lies in Section 2, Lot 459 — one of the only marked graves among the victims. Eula Phillips lies unmarked in the Old Grounds. Mollie Smith, Eliza Shelley, Irene Cross, and eleven-year-old Mary Ramey were buried in the cemetery's segregated \"colored grounds,\" their graves largely unmarked to this day; where Gracie Vance and Orange Washington rest is not reliably recorded. Say the eight names together, in the one place that holds this story: Mollie Smith, Eliza Shelley, Irene Cross, Mary Ramey, Gracie Vance, Orange Washington, Susan Hancock, Eula Phillips.","lore":false},{"heading":"A city that wanted to forget","text":"By the end of 1885, contemporary reports claimed that roughly four hundred men had been arrested or questioned in connection with the panic, many of them Black; no conviction ultimately stood. After Christmas 1885 came curfews, armed patrols, an expanded police force, and then silence: the attacks simply stopped. When Nathan Elgin — the young, Wheatville-raised cook whose right foot shared the distinctive missing-little-toe characteristic seen in the bloody print — was killed by a police officer in February 1886, the murders never resumed; but he was never charged or tried, and the case is formally unsolved. Three years before Whitechapel, this was arguably America's first tracked serial-killer panic — yet the city let most of its victims lie unmarked, and remembered the year mostly through folklore.","lore":false},{"heading":"Visiting with respect","text":"Oakwood is an active, historic cemetery. Visit during its posted hours and in daylight, keep to the paths and roadways, speak quietly, and leave every marker and grave exactly as you find it — this is a place of mourning first and history second. The Oakwood Cemetery Chapel serves as an interpretive center, and the nonprofit Save Austin's Cemeteries researches these very burials, including work toward identifying graves in the segregated grounds; supporting that work is the most direct way to turn a tour into a memorial. Before you go, say their names once more.","lore":false}],"survivors":[],"siteToday":"Oakwood Cemetery remains an active historic cemetery at 1601 Navasota Street, about 1.3 miles east of the Capitol.","loreNotes":[{"title":"Lore: the moonlight towers","text":"Austin legend says the city raised its 165-foot \"moonlight towers\" to strip the midnight killer of his darkness. The chronology disproves it: the murders ended in December 1885, and Austin bought its 31 second-hand towers from Detroit and erected them in 1894–95 — nearly a decade later. Fifteen still stand, given a historical marker in 1970, listed on the National Register in 1976, and designated State Antiquities Landmarks in 1981; one of them starred in Dazed and Confused. The towers are not why the killings stopped — but the killings became the origin story Austinites tell about the towers, a lesson in how a city remembers trauma by rearranging it into legend."}]}}};
